        "id": "s4_floral_formula",
        "kind": "boxed_final",
        "label": "Floral formula",
        "marks": 1,
        "lines": [
          {
            "text": "Br Ebrl ⊕ ⚥ P(3+3) A3+3 G(3)",
            "style": "boxed"
          },
          "Underline the G — the ovary is superior.",
          "P = perianth. The calyx and corolla look alike, so",
          "they are written as one whorl of six tepals, not",
          "as K and C."
        ],
        "why": "Liliaceae has no separate calyx and corolla, so the formula uses P instead of K and C, and that single letter is the family signature.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing K(3) C(3) instead of P(3+3).",
          "Bracketing the androecium. The six stamens are free, so A3+3 takes no bracket."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "When sepals and petals cannot be told apart, they become one perianth: P, not K and C.",
        "margin_note": "One line for the formula. Underline the G by hand.",
        "mark_note": "Formula"
